name: instrument-sharpened
kind: inferred
shape: categorical
face: in
membrane: false
note: |
  Fires when the measuring apparatus itself gains discrimination — the harness,
  the board, or a checker can now tell apart two outcomes it previously scored
  the same. Not "a bug was fixed" (that is gap-closed) and not "a new thing
  works" (capability-gained): the SUBJECT is the instrument, and what changed is
  what it is able to notice.

  Attention-worthy because a blunt instrument reports success at exactly the
  moments it should report nothing. Every such conflation is a green that was
  never earned, and it is invisible by construction — the board cannot show you
  a distinction it does not draw. Sharpening one usually turns greens red, and
  those reds are the instrument working, not a regression.

  Canonical instance: a negative test pinning no diagnostic passed when the
  program failed for ANY reason, so "rejected for the reason I pinned" and
  "broke somehow" scored identically (MUST_ERROR walls, 6f8dea46).

  Detector — negative tests that still prove nothing about WHY they fail:
    for d in $(find tests/regression -name MUST_ERROR -exec dirname {} \;); do
      [ -s "$d/expected_error.txt" ] || [ -f "$d/expected_patterns.txt" ] \
        || [ -f "$d/post.sh" ] \
        || grep -qE "^(CONTAINS|ERROR_AT) " "$d/EXPECT" 2>/dev/null || echo "$d"
    done
route: surface, wake
